WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.740
- Hi everyone. Good afternoon and welcome.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.450
My name is Priya Natarajan.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.720
I'm an astrophysicist with
strong intellectual interests

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.400
in the history and philosophy of science,

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.680
and I serve as the current director

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.070
of Yale's flagship
interdisciplinary Franke Program

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.230
in Science and the Humanities.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.850
It's my absolute pleasure today

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.670
to start this wonderful event

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.520
by first recognizing our benefactors, Mr.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.010
and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.350
Thanks to their generosity, this program

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.780
and many other initiatives
at Yale have benefited

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.420
from their vision to foster scholarship

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.040
that breaks disciplinary boundaries

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.073
and generates radical new lenses
to view the world through.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.430
Meanwhile, before we start today's event,

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.520
I am required to give you this notice.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.830
This event is being
recorded and photographed

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.740
for educational, archival
and promotional purposes,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.320
including use in print on
the internet and other forms

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.250
of media.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.080
By attending this event today, you agree

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.720
to the possibility of your
voice or your likeness captured

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.180
by these means and used for such purposes

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.040
without compensation to you

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.120
and hereby waive any related
right to privacy or publicity.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.860
So before I introduce our
fantastic speaker this evening,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.640
let me remind you that we
have reception upstairs

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.250
after the talk in the
student lounge immediately

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.083
after the event,

00:01:27.083 --> 00:01:29.820
so you are all invited
to please come join us.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.860
There's some really yummy food there.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.693
So now onto today's event,

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.470
I can promise you it'll be a real treat.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.660
This evening, we are
gathered for a book talk

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.190
by one of our own,

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:44.220
the incredible mind whom
we are fortunate to have

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.160
on our faculty at Yale,
professor Jing Tsu.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.350
Professor Tsu, the John
M. Schiff Professor

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.210
of East Asian Languages and literatures

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.990
and Comparative Literature at
Yale is a cultural historian,

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:58.920
literary scholar of modern
China at Yale University.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.750
The first tenured professor appointed

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.700
in East Asian languages and literatures

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.370
and comparative literature,
her research spans literature,

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:08.850
linguistics, science and
technology, internationalization,

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.130
regionalization, regionalism, sorry,

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.130
and globalization and US China relations.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:19.050
She's a member and former chair
2014 to 2021 of the Council

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.540
on East Asian studies and
a senior research fellow

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.420
at the MacMillan Center for
International and Area Studies

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:25.680
and an affiliate faculty

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.680
at the Jackson School of Global Affairs.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.590
Professor Tsu has written
for The New York Times,

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.720
New York Times Book Review,
The Financial Times,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.540
and has authored or co-edited five books.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.750
Her most recent book which is
what we're gonna be hearing

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:43.050
about today, "Kingdom of
Characters: The Language Revolution

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.570
That Made China Modern" was named

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.360
among the hundred notable books of 2022

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:53.100
by The New York Times and the
best non-fiction of 2022 list

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.510
in The Washington Post.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.760
It's been reviewed extensively.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.360
Pretty much every major
publication has reviewed it

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.560
across the world, including
The New York Times,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.020
The Wall Street Journal,
Financial Times, The New Yorker,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.140
Economist, Wired, Science, Nature,

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.090
The Guardian, Times Literary Supplement,

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.590
London Review of Books,

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:18.300
and the South China Morning
Post, and of course the NRC

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.080
in Holland and the Frankfurter
Allemand in Germany.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.300
This book tells the story of how China

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.270
and its language entered the
information age dominated

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.070
by the Western alphabet.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.590
In spring 2023,

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.867
Jing Tsu is delivering Yale's
William Clyde DeVane lectures,

00:03:34.867 --> 00:03:37.200
"China in Six Keys."

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.840
Jing is widely recognized
for her original approach,

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.230
interdisciplinary synthesis
and perspective on China.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:47.520
She has held fellowships
and honors from a multitude

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.070
of places, including the
Society of Fellows at Harvard,

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.390
the Woodrow Wilson Foundation,

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.760
the Center for Advanced Study
in the Behavioral Sciences

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.610
at Stanford, the Alexander
von Humboldt Foundation,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:57.960
the Radcliffe Institute,

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.600
New Directions Fellowship
from the Mellon Foundation,

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.220
and the Institute for Advanced Study

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:06.360
and the John Simon Guggenheim Foundation.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:07.380
I don't need to tell you this.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.900
She's an extremely distinguished scholar,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.580
whose work we are really
excited to hear more about.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.390
Before we start the event and
I invite Jing to the stage,

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:21.060
I want to mention a couple
of our ongoing projects

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.500
and upcoming events.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.960
So we have a learning by doing project,

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.110
which is we are collecting
little simple science videos

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:31.650
of little experiments that can
be done at home for children

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:34.470
around the world who can
do little experiments just

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:35.700
to fulfill their curiosity.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.430
So if you would like to
be a contributor and send

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:41.430
in a video, please go ahead
and take a look at our website.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.870
We would be delighted.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.910
The next announcement I want to make,

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.880
quick one is next Wednesday at 4:00 PM,

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.660
we have a film screening of
"The Lost Leonardo" followed

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.090
by a full post film moderated discussion

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:57.660
with Dianne Modestini,
the famed art conservator,

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:01.096
whose restoration work
actually led to the discovery

00:05:01.096 --> 00:05:04.620
of the artwork and was
critical in establishing

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.480
that it was a Leonardo.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.250
So yeah, please mark your calendars,

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.140
next Wednesday at 4:00 PM.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.480
And on Tuesday April 18th,

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.250
we have another wonderful book talk

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.710
by one of our own, professor Beverly Gage,

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.637
who will be talking to
us about her recent book,

00:05:18.637 --> 00:05:21.210
"G-Man: J. Edgar Hoover and the Making

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.160
of the American Century."

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.387
And on April 26th we have,

00:05:25.387 --> 00:05:28.200
"We Carry Our World Within
Us: Cellular Structures

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.080
That Allow Us To Live in A
Very Hostile Environment."

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.150
I want to often read out these 'cause

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:36.780
to show you how interdisciplinary
the offerings are.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.570
So please mark your
calendars and I look forward

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:42.120
to seeing you at many of these events.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.350
So, it's a particular pleasure
for me as Jing also happens

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.930
to be a close friend
so without further ado,

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:50.670
Jing, the floor is yours.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:51.995
Thank you so much for coming.

00:05:51.995 --> 00:05:54.995
(audience clapping)

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.500
- Thank you very much, Priya.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.540
I have to first thank the Franke program

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:01.770
for having me today.

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:04.410
I'm not sure if any talk
I can deliver can live up

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.470
to the very generous
introduction from Priya.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.930
But as you know, we're friends. (chuckles)

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.000
In any case, it's a delight to be here.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.980
I feel that it's long overdue

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:15.540
since the book came out a year ago

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.360
and I thank also Ellis Kaplan,

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.700
director of the humanities
center last year,

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:24.063
Whitney Humanities Center,
who invited me to come speak.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.500
So today, as I've given a
lot of talks on this book,

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.960
I thought I'd share with you here

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.260
in some ways the path that led me to it.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.780
That's why it's called
"Behind Kingdom Characters"

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:37.920
in some ways, and I'll take you

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.593
through this particular
thread in the book.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:46.140
There's really three points of genesis

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:49.380
and one actually brings
me back to my roots

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:50.940
as a literary scholar.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.040
As a student of monetized literature,

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.310
any student will have
read all the writings,

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:58.443
if not most by this man, Hu Shi.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:03.870
He was a proponent of
using vernacular speech

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:07.620
to replace on the elite
literary classical Chinese

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.660
in the early 20th century.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:10.650
This was a movement

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:14.730
that really revolutionized modern China

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:17.520
because it gave language
back to the people,

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:18.930
the power of literacy

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.600
and this is really the beginning of it.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.600
But of all the things that
you will read by Hu Shi,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.870
there's this one here
that's often unnoticed

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.630
and it took me years until
I embarked in this book

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:34.350
to realize that all the evidence
and the cues were there.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.060
I just didn't quite see it

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.260
when I was strictly a
student of literature.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.890
So this is a preface that he wrote

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:46.740
to this scheme on Chinese characters,

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:48.840
which I will talk about in a bit.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.690
He talks about, of all
things, not vernacular

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:55.173
versus elite language, but
the idea of organization.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:57.247
And he says here,

00:07:57.247 --> 00:08:01.260
"To order is to find a
logical threat in chaos."

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:04.800
And here, he's actually
commenting on this immense task

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.980
that Chinese were faced with
in the early 20th century.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.860
How do they actually preserve and hold on

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:14.520
to the written heritage, given
that there's an onslaught

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:17.040
of Westernization and
a pressure monetization

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:20.070
that was basically making
old knowledge seem old

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:23.883
and unpopular and very kind of calcified?

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.494
But then when he talks
about this, he said,

00:08:26.494 --> 00:08:27.510
"You know, what's really important,

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:30.720
the hard task is really
this very pedestrian,

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:32.790
very mundane thing to do

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:34.620
which is to make sure
that you can find things

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.300
where you find them."

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:38.007
And that is tedious for sure

00:08:38.007 --> 00:08:39.950
and he talks about how this is really kind

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:42.570
of like a service industry part

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:44.400
of modern Chinese literature,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.890
that there's gonna be people
who will have to do it

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:49.350
and they will be like
the saviors and martyrs

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:53.340
for literature, but of
course not Hu Shi himself,

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.290
although he will support them.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:56.123
And he said,

00:08:56.123 --> 00:08:59.460
"People who engage in this
kind of painstaking labor do it

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:01.260
so that other people don't have to."

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:06.630
And so regardless, oh sorry.

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:09.423
I'm not sure why this just fell out.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:19.110
Is it coming up? Great. Sorry.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:22.440
Now you see what I'm
reading from. (laughs)

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.090
Sorry for the momentary blindness.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:26.820
So regardless of what people do,

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:30.720
the scholarly world is
utterly indebted to them.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.650
So it's this group of people
that I got very interested in.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:39.650
Who is this invisible community

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.540
that managed to carry
modern Chinese literature,

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:46.260
the very language that made
up literature, on their backs,

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:48.480
but who went utterly unrecognized?

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:49.860
And you know, as Hu Shi said,

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.443
they're basically doomed to anonymity.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:56.760
So that was my first thread
right there, my first clue,

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.623
and I tucked this away in my mind.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:02.460
And about 10 years ago when I
was working into the history

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:04.140
of science and technology,

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:06.270
I decided to educate myself

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:10.590
by reading these very famous seminal works

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:13.267
by Joseph Needham called,

00:10:13.267 --> 00:10:16.082
"Science and Civilization in China."

00:10:16.082 --> 00:10:17.460
It was about 26 volumes.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:19.770
Don't worry, I didn't
get through all of 'em.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:23.640
26 volumes and Joseph Needham
was a towering figure.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:28.640
And in 1956, in volume two,
he made these references

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:31.313
in footnotes about the Chinese language.

00:10:31.313 --> 00:10:34.290
And you can see that this
is where this towering mind

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:35.123
at the time,

00:10:35.123 --> 00:10:39.900
he had doubts about the
fundamental task he was undertaking

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:41.100
which was to justify

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:44.460
and explain how China had always
had science and technology,

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.380
right, to refute the fact that just

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:48.840
because that scientific
revolution took place

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:50.160
in the West, does it mean

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:52.920
that China didn't have
their own accomplishments,

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:54.663
scientific and technological?

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:58.950
But he had a doubt when
it came to the language.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:02.797
And he will quote other people
in these footnotes and say,

00:11:02.797 --> 00:11:04.540
"Well, some people say that it's something

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:06.480
about the Chinese language

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.370
that hampers through thinking, right,

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.980
that kind of skews the
way or limits the way

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:13.620
or charts a particular
way of thinking because

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:17.070
of the character idiographic
writing system they had."

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.590
But clearly, he had doubts
because he did not put that

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:20.970
in a main body

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:23.467
and there was yet another
footnote that says,

00:11:23.467 --> 00:11:28.230
"Well, Chinese seem
particularly well-positioned

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:32.310
to think comminatory logic
by virtue of the fact

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.920
that character writing
are made of parts rather

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:39.147
than alphabetic letters
in a linear rollout."

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:43.500
So that was my second clue and
I kinda wondered about that.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.450
Is it true at the time?

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:48.300
Is it true that alphabetical thinking,

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:53.040
the alphabetic mind in
some ways was more amenable

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:54.270
to modernization?

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:57.090
Was it more superior?
Was it more accessible?

00:11:57.090 --> 00:12:00.600
Was it easier than idiographic thinking

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.630
or thinking in the Chinese
character language?

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:05.547
And of course, when I
looked into the literature

00:12:05.547 --> 00:12:08.060
and the scholarship, the
history that have been written

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:11.860
in my field, it wasn't very
helpful because whenever we talk

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:13.500
about Chinese language,

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:16.170
it was very much cast in a frame of, ah,

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:18.573
superiority versus inferiority,

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:23.130
the inferiorization of
Chinese language and culture

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:25.230
and the way that we put them as primitive

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:28.950
or more backwards as opposed
to Western civilization, right?

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:31.860
Which is actually a
relatively recent hierarchy

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:35.253
that happened in the 19th
century with the revolution.

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:39.810
And so I looked in other places,

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.460
hoping that there were other disciplines

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:43.260
that could give me a clue.

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:48.180
And this is around 2015 where
I then came across a subfield

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:50.553
in neuroscience, which was a study,

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:53.523
the neuroscience of reading.

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:56.310
So writing system, you know,

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:59.700
we might not think about
this, is actually very young.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:02.070
It basically developed
within a blink of an eye

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:04.530
in the larger shape of evolution, right?

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:07.950
About 5,000 years, 5,500 years old.

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:11.070
So, it's not really possible
that evolutionarily,

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:12.510
we could have adapted,

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:13.980
our brains could've adapted so quickly

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:16.440
that we have a particular set of neurons

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:17.840
that just deal with writing.

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:23.580
So it turns out they're built
on a prior infrastructure

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:27.090
of pattern recognition,
of identifying, you know,

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:28.650
where the horizon meets,

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:30.600
where there's intersection in the roads.

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:32.000
You will also know that when you look out

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:34.938
at the expanse of the ocean, you know,

00:13:34.938 --> 00:13:37.170
you normally fix your
gaze somewhere, right?

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:38.910
So that's what we're kind of fine-tuned

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:42.180
for evolutionary purposes so
that we can find our way home

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:45.060
after we, you know, hit
that tiger over the head

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.760
or deer or try to carry
it back to our caves.

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:49.413
So it makes a lot of sense.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.590
And it took me to Marina Delray

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:55.290
to visit a neuroscientist
who were the first

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.420
to talk about pattern recognition

00:13:57.420 --> 00:14:01.410
and the visual image
processing, Irving Biederman,

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:04.500
whom actually, I was sad to
find out just the other day

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:06.393
that he passed away in 2022.

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:08.760
And so, this train of thought

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:10.920
then led me down another rabbit hole

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:13.170
because it turns out
according to neuroscientists,

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:16.950
there are different ways
and different startup costs

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:20.550
for reading the alphabet or
reading autographic writing,

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:24.300
but eventually it all comes out
pretty much the same, right?

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:27.030
Literacy in literacy, we learn to accustom

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:29.820
and to connect patterns in such a way

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:32.430
that they become conventionalized
and internalized,

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:34.440
and that's when we read them.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:36.120
So, this is kinda the bottom line,

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:38.550
but I'm still not quite satisfied.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:41.530
So, what is it about the Chinese language

00:14:43.689 --> 00:14:45.930
that's so mystified for centuries

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:50.930
that baffled the Western
missionaries since the 16th century

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:55.770
that have given rise to
accusations and assumptions

00:14:55.770 --> 00:15:00.120
of inferiority, of what
held the Chinese back

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.100
in the 20th century, so on and so forth?

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:04.200
And the criticism was not just coming

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:07.500
from Westerners themselves,
but also the Chinese.

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:11.250
They too came to see, mainly
because their interaction

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:14.130
with the Western alphabet, how cumbersome,

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:17.100
in some ways how unfriendly
the Chinese language was

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:18.690
to modernity.

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:21.870
So it was my goal, then, to set out

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:26.160
to prove or to figure
out, is that really true

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:27.783
and if so, why.

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:33.300
And there's also a more
direct route to this

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:36.397
because when I was working
on my last book in 2010,

00:15:36.397 --> 00:15:39.210
"Sound and Script in Chinese Diaspora",

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:42.330
one of my favorite writers,
Lin Yutang, turned out,

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:46.593
his lifelong passion was to
build a Chinese typewriter.

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:49.050
At the time, I didn't
know what to make of this

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:50.220
and quite disappointed, of course.

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:51.150
As a student of literature,

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:53.790
you imagine these writers
just locked themselves away

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:55.740
in an attic and to, you know,

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:57.870
listen to their muse and write away

00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:01.290
but he had a very mechanical
engineer-like mind.

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:03.367
And so, I flagged it
in this chapter called,

00:16:03.367 --> 00:16:06.120
"Lin Yutang's Typewriter" and I vowed

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:08.103
that I would come back to it,

00:16:08.103 --> 00:16:10.440
and so hence about a decade later,

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:12.303
after going through many other,

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:16.710
I think digressions and detours and many,

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.950
many archives and delving
deep into this and talking

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:22.470
to many people in the industry

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:24.420
'cause that's what this project took

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:25.820
and that's where we are now.

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:29.940
So, let me just go back to this.

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:32.490
You will see that the
footnote is from an article

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:33.450
that Hu Shi wrote.

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:35.100
I mentioned that's a preface.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:35.933
It's a preface

00:16:35.933 --> 00:16:39.270
to something called the
"Four Corner Index System."

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:42.660
And it's by a man, Wang
Yunwu, who was not alone

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:43.980
in this endeavor.

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:47.250
So what this meant was,
(indistinct) organization,

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:50.430
just as in the alphabet,
you see in dictionaries,

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:52.620
this all organized by ABCD,

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:55.290
you might not appreciate
the power of the alphabet.

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:59.970
But this incredible ease and efficiency

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:03.390
of self-organization has meant a lot

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:06.090
of different other kinds
of conveniences, right?

00:17:06.090 --> 00:17:10.410
You know, B never comes before
A, you know, T comes after S.

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:13.050
So this predictability
gives an immense power

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:15.360
in simply organizing libraries.

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:17.490
You can look up anything
in the dictionary,

00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:20.190
anything to catalog, telephone directories

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:22.710
and even early days of computing.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:27.710
So, the fact that you just
need to learn 26 symbols

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:31.020
and then you can construct
any word you want

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:33.870
in the English language, in
all Indo-European languages,

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:37.503
out of those 26 symbols,
it's actually remarkable.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:42.360
Now, if I can shift you to
look at Chinese characters,

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:46.680
there's about 80,000,
depending on who you ask.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:50.100
Of course, not all of 'em are
being used at any given time.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.710
And in fact for modern day literacy,

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:56.760
you probably need three, four,
three to 5,000 characters.

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:58.650
Still a lot compared to 26.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:17:59.837
I can tell you stories of, you know,

00:17:59.837 --> 00:18:02.130
one of the strongest memories, growing up,

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:05.910
is writing these characters
over and over again.

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:10.260
You would get anywhere between
15 to 35, 40 of them a day

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:13.590
in school and the bulk of your homework,

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:14.550
at least back in the 80s,

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:17.190
would be to just repeat
them over and over again

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:19.203
to commit them to muscle memory.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:25.460
So, what this man did in
"Four Corner Index System" was

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:28.350
to figure out how to use characters

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:31.443
to give them this kind
of indexical function.

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:35.340
And what he proposed was, actually,

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:39.583
lemme just skip here a
little bit, about here is,

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:44.070
to put it very simply there,

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.380
he basically takes parts of characters

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:48.090
'cause you see that Chinese characters are

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:49.620
in a square space.

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:52.890
So roughly, he can divide
it up into four quadrants.

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:54.960
And you can use the corner,

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:57.630
if you identify the contour of
what the character looks like

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:00.900
in the corner, you assign a number to it.

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:04.350
So his idea is that, okay,
there were four numbers.

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:07.080
You can tag a character on all four sides

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:09.840
and that will help you zoom in
and hone in on the character

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:11.820
that you're looking for.

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:14.820
Now this is very
important because why else

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:16.830
and how else would you look for characters

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:19.743
to fish 'em out of, let's say,
80 thousands or thousands?

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:23.310
So he was not alone in this.

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:26.257
There was Chen Guanyao, who
wrote the "Standard Radicals",

00:19:26.257 --> 00:19:29.130
"Simplified Characters Index."

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:31.627
There was Shen Zurong and Hu Qingsheng's,

00:19:31.627 --> 00:19:33.930
"Look-Up in 12 Character Strokes."

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:36.360
So this is from 1920s and 1940s.

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:38.760
There was a massive, massive race

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:42.030
to find what's called a
character retrieval system,

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:47.030
the golden key that will
unlock all these doors,

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.293
and the list goes on and on.

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:53.040
Now, Wang Yunwu was the one
that's highlighted in red,

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:57.330
but he was not the only one,
although the most successful.

00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:59.880
In fact, one of the pleasures
of working on this book is

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:03.300
to basically engage with
these human characters

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:05.370
behind every Chinese character

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:07.830
that made their preservation possible.

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:11.100
And when you get to know
them, you understand why.

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:13.710
For instance, this man, Zhang Feng,

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:17.610
a young man at the time, had the audacity

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:22.050
of perhaps having hope of
challenging Wang Yunwu's system.

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:22.883
Now, Wang Yunwu

00:20:22.883 --> 00:20:25.560
at the time was already
a publishing maverick.

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:27.690
He was very well connected.

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:29.790
He claims to have been an autodidact,

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:32.820
taught himself by reading
"Encyclopedia Britannica"

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:34.380
from page to page.

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:36.270
He got up at four every morning,

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:38.880
drank gallons of water and
go on like a brisk walk.

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:41.520
It was to create allure around himself,

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:43.820
so obviously he was also
a marketing maverick.

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:47.460
Now Zhang Feng was this young man,

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:52.230
who obviously was very smart
and he had a great idea

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:55.470
about tagging characters, not by numbers,

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.490
but by looking at its shape

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:02.730
and he organized a kind of
simpler system this way.

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:05.460
And so, he was all but
20 something years old

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:08.093
and decided he wants to
challenge Wang Yunwu.

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:12.600
And he made the mistake,
one could say in retrospect,

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:15.420
by issuing a public
challenge in a newspaper,

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:17.100
asking him to almost like a duel.

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:20.610
I have no idea what that would've
looked like in real life.

00:21:20.610 --> 00:21:22.350
I dunno if he'd throw
characters at each other

00:21:22.350 --> 00:21:24.120
or pieces of paper at each other.

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:28.240
Then in the case, he issued
that challenge and he did a lot.

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:31.740
He carried his samples,

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:33.660
he gave them to anyone
he could teach it to.

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:34.830
He really tried to publicize,

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:36.900
but it was only one man, right?

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:39.450
And he could not possibly
defeat the entire,

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:43.500
publicity machinery that Wang
Yunwu had at his fingertips

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:45.690
because he did something very clever,

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:47.700
which was at the time,
he was a main editor

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:51.510
for the biggest publishing house
in China, Commercial Press,

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:52.773
which still exists today.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:57.120
And Commercial Press put out
all these reference works,

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:00.180
which was actually not
common prior to 20th century,

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:03.240
but modern reference works
like the dictionary, thesaurus,

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:04.290
et cetera, et cetera.

00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:07.440
And to use any of them,

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:10.413
you had to learn Wang
Yunwu's four corner system.

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:12.173
I can tell you,

00:22:12.173 --> 00:22:14.760
the four corner system is
not easy to use (laughs)

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:17.820
and yet, it still persisted
when I was in graduate school

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:21.670
at Harvard in 2000s where

00:22:22.617 --> 00:22:24.180
that's still how you have
to look up certain things

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:25.683
in the old catalog system.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:28.200
So Wang Yunwu was quite successful.

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:31.380
He basically did the one thing I think

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:34.890
that would bruise any young ego.

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:37.053
He just simply ignored Zhang Feng.

00:22:38.010 --> 00:22:41.430
And Zhang Feng pretty
much fell into anonymity,

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:44.760
but his scheme is actually
one that won a lot of,

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:47.103
I think, confirmation much later on.

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:52.650
So what's really at stake
when we talk about characters?

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:57.060
Well, there's also the
added external perception

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:59.370
of Chinese characters
that didn't really help,

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:03.900
didn't really help highlight
this intrinsically materialist,

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:07.680
technological challenge that
posed for the modern age

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:11.370
because since to 17, actually
16, but more famously,

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:15.150
17th century, Europeans
have been fantasizing

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:19.080
and imagining what Chinese
writing was all about, right,

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:20.460
the exotic character.

00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:21.647
Here, this is a man,

00:23:21.647 --> 00:23:24.300
Athanasius Kircher who's very well known.

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:26.730
He wrote this book, "China Illustrata",

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:30.870
which was published in
1667 where he shows, right,

00:23:30.870 --> 00:23:33.000
that characters written
this fair ornate way,

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:34.890
they're so in tune in nature

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:39.090
that they're literally put
together by turtles or bugs,

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:41.440
if you can see that, if
you can see that below.

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:46.953
And most Europeans who actually
had not traveled abroad,

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:50.610
did this just go out?

00:23:50.610 --> 00:23:52.140
Is this still on? Okay.

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:54.930
Most Europeans who
actually had not traveled

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:57.120
abroad took his word for it and he

00:23:57.120 --> 00:23:59.100
in turn took other people's word for it

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:00.777
because there were
missionaries who went to China

00:24:00.777 --> 00:24:03.090
and actually tried to work
with the Chinese language

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:03.993
on the ground,

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:08.700
but Kircher had a particular
imagination about what it was.

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:13.620
And this kind of imagination or fantasy

00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:17.490
about the Chinese language
will persist down to our day.

00:24:17.490 --> 00:24:19.740
But I just wanna point
out how powerful it was

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:23.760
that external perception had
begun this other kind of lore

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:25.110
about the Chinese language.

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:30.450
And I also wanna say as a
footnote that it turns out,

00:24:30.450 --> 00:24:34.230
it was not completely
Kircher's imagination.

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:37.890
He didn't just pull this out
of a hat because at the time,

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:40.680
there were certain books
that were translated,

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:43.110
or actually brought back to Europe,

00:24:43.110 --> 00:24:45.123
putting libraries, et cetera.

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:47.220
And one of those books

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:50.940
that we are quite sure he
saw was this particular one

00:24:50.940 --> 00:24:54.723
which was known as a
popular almanac at the time.

00:24:56.190 --> 00:24:57.480
It's hard to describe what it is,

00:24:57.480 --> 00:25:00.810
but it's sort of a for
layman, popular usage,

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:02.220
and it was to sort of decorate

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:04.890
and kinda exaggerate sort
of Chinese character writing

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:07.350
for decorative purposes largely.

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:10.290
And then so, any Chinese
wouldn't take this seriously

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:12.390
as high knowledge.

00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:14.130
But of course for Europeans
who were just kind

00:25:14.130 --> 00:25:15.810
of bringing these books back,

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:18.660
they didn't really have
the context to evaluate.

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:20.820
So what Kircher turned out,

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:24.990
where he got it from was simply
this very popular almanac

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:28.533
that is not really part of
the Chinese language theory.

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:33.660
Believe that as it may,
this imagination persisted.

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:36.300
How do you deal with these thousands,

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:38.610
tens of thousands of characters?

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:43.610
Well, in 1897, the first
person would try to put Chinese

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:50.070
into a typewriter, was this Presbyterian,

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:52.530
American Presbyterian missionary.

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:53.820
So I wanna extend with the fact

00:25:53.820 --> 00:25:55.140
that this Chinese language,

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:57.150
the technologization of Chinese language,

00:25:57.150 --> 00:25:58.500
was not just a Chinese affair.

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:00.870
It's not just the Chinese
who were concerned about it.

00:26:00.870 --> 00:26:04.410
Outsiders, foreigners,
Westerners were equally concerned

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:07.800
because they want a way
to break into China,

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:11.220
into its market to win the
hearts and minds of its peoples,

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:15.150
to proselytize, to convert
them, et cetera, et cetera.

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:19.770
So, Sheffield's answer was,
well, kind of workmanlike.

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:20.603
He decides,

00:26:20.603 --> 00:26:22.980
"Well, of course you need
to basically have a key

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:24.333
for every character and you have a place

00:26:24.333 --> 00:26:26.430
with every whole character."

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:29.100
So he put thousands on
what is basically like,

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:32.280
a flat disk drum, and
you find the character.

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:33.840
So all the little dots that you see

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:36.840
on it are actually
supposed to be characters.

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:38.760
And then you sort of crank that,

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:42.060
use that crane and you somehow
line it up with the needle

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:44.610
on top and then do this very laborious,

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:46.800
it's basically like a
three, four step process,

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:49.013
but you can imagine a
person who's doing it,

00:26:49.013 --> 00:26:51.960
it's almost like rubbing your
tummy and patting your head

00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:55.110
at the same time because you
have to rotate on one hand,

00:26:55.110 --> 00:26:58.230
but then also kind of push
and pull with the other.

00:26:58.230 --> 00:27:01.290
They were very physically challenging,

00:27:01.290 --> 00:27:02.760
but Sheffield kinda meant it that way.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:03.593
He said,

00:27:03.593 --> 00:27:04.426
"Because, you know,

00:27:04.426 --> 00:27:06.750
the physical labor will
teach those lazy people how

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:08.850
to actually remember, learn characters."

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:11.460
He's talking about his
fellow missionaries, right?

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:12.960
That they actually have
to commit characters

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:15.183
to memory the same way that Chinese do.

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:20.580
But nonetheless, it was not
a very imaginative solution

00:27:20.580 --> 00:27:23.010
because this was obviously cumbersome,

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:26.430
difficult to use even though
it seemed like a giant leap

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:27.513
forward at the time,

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:32.580
because this is what we're dealing with.

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:37.470
Now, this is a keyboard
tray. It's inverted, right?

00:27:37.470 --> 00:27:40.170
So you see the characters
in this sort of mirror image

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:43.290
and each of those characters are carved

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:45.003
into these lead slugs.

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:48.660
And every time you want
to print something,

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:50.640
you would take out a tray,

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:54.107
you arrange these characters
just like the way you have it

00:27:54.107 --> 00:27:58.233
in the text and then you
use it to print that way.

00:27:59.494 --> 00:28:01.997
And China actually
invented this technology

00:28:01.997 --> 00:28:05.340
in the 11th century, the movable type.

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:08.793
At the time, it was kind of
made of clay, not quite lead.

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:11.790
But this technology of literally,

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:14.810
you have to carve each character
into a slug and then use it

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:17.100
to type and rearrange it for a text

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:19.940
and then do it all over again
to fish 'em out, you know,

00:28:19.940 --> 00:28:22.950
of a storage and then do it
like over and over again,

00:28:22.950 --> 00:28:25.140
this was actually a main technique

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:29.013
for printing up to mid 20th century.

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:30.933
Pretty remarkable.

00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:35.070
Was it cumbersome for Chinese? Yes.

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:37.350
But they also found a way
around this such that,

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:38.640
if you can believe it,

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:42.120
it was faster for them
to melt down everything

00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:45.460
at the end of the day and
then recast the next day

00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:47.880
than it is for them to try

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:51.270
to locate these characters, right?

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:52.977
So the main issue really is,

00:28:52.977 --> 00:28:56.250
and who should had absolutely
right, how to select,

00:28:56.250 --> 00:28:58.980
retrieve and locate characters.

00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:02.850
That was gonna be key to almost
everything in modernization

00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:05.220
because if you don't
modernize your language,

00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:07.413
nothing else will be able to.

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:15.420
So, that previous image of the type,

00:29:15.420 --> 00:29:19.803
the tray of types is actually
a closeup of this typewriter.

00:29:20.730 --> 00:29:25.140
There's three of them in the
United States, three existing.

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:28.350
This one is in the
Museum of Chinese America

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:30.240
in New York City.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:33.600
Another one's in the
Huntington Library in Pasadena,

00:29:33.600 --> 00:29:37.023
and the third one we believe
is in private collection.

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:43.020
So this is called a Shu-style,
this Chinese typewriter,

00:29:43.020 --> 00:29:46.470
because this man Shu actually
did not invent it himself,

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:49.080
but he built it based on the model

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:52.290
by a previous Chinese typewriter inventor.

00:29:52.290 --> 00:29:54.720
After Sheffield in 1897,

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:57.750
it was this young man, Zhou Houkun,

00:29:57.750 --> 00:30:00.093
who actually went to MIT.

00:30:01.919 --> 00:30:02.880
He's actually a second class,

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:05.670
but one of the first wave of
Chinese students who were sent

00:30:05.670 --> 00:30:08.643
abroad to study MIT, Cornell,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:10.890
And he was a double major.

00:30:10.890 --> 00:30:13.980
He was actually the first
graduate aeronautical engineering,

00:30:13.980 --> 00:30:17.100
which was a brand new
program MIT at the time,

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:18.273
present day avionics.

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:22.740
And this was also not supposed
to be his senior thesis.

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:25.470
His senior thesis was
about how to make a bamboo

00:30:25.470 --> 00:30:27.480
as strong as concrete.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:31.650
But, as with my writer, Lin Yutang,

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:34.110
this was his passion and he worked

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:36.710
on this whenever he was not
working on his homework.

00:30:37.650 --> 00:30:39.990
So the Shu-style Chinese
typewriter works with,

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:42.690
you see that there's a
tray of slugs, right?

00:30:42.690 --> 00:30:46.260
And every time you moved
this, there's no keyboard.

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:48.330
That's the important
thing to bear in mind.

00:30:48.330 --> 00:30:51.690
There's a forest of keys, but no keyboard.

00:30:51.690 --> 00:30:53.310
So you can't really type it.

00:30:53.310 --> 00:30:55.320
You can only kind of locate it

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:57.690
through kinda like Sheffield's typewriter,

00:30:57.690 --> 00:31:01.323
which Zhou Houkun actually
took a lot of inspiration from.

00:31:01.323 --> 00:31:02.970
Zhou Houkun recognized

00:31:02.970 --> 00:31:06.420
that each character would
still have his own slug,

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:10.470
its own type, that characters
cannot be divided up.

00:31:10.470 --> 00:31:13.470
Because he inherited this
thinking, and this is true,

00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:17.730
Chinese characters, traditionally,

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:20.550
there's only one part of
it that can be singled out

00:31:20.550 --> 00:31:22.350
for indexical purposes,

00:31:22.350 --> 00:31:25.000
but it doesn't quite fit
the purpose of modern needs.

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:30.243
So this is Zhou Houkun, the MIT student,

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:34.230
and his solution to a force
of keys was basically,

00:31:34.230 --> 00:31:35.760
he only had a blueprint.

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:40.680
We know that he built a
typewriter, but he was a star MIT.

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:43.710
He had job offers in the United States,

00:31:43.710 --> 00:31:46.270
but he decided that he
would go back to China

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:49.177
because he had thought,

00:31:49.177 --> 00:31:52.080
"Airplanes is not what China needed now

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:54.030
where there's not even cars,

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:57.600
but if you could bring back a way

00:31:57.600 --> 00:32:00.120
of printing Chinese characters,

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:02.610
of mechanizing the Chinese language so

00:32:02.610 --> 00:32:05.340
that you can make print
and literacy accessible,

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:08.970
that would be the one
measure that will be critical

00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:10.647
to China's national survival."

00:32:12.090 --> 00:32:13.762
So my favorite story
about this is actually,

00:32:13.762 --> 00:32:15.060
Zhou Houkun comes back.

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:17.670
He goes back to Shanghai where he's from

00:32:17.670 --> 00:32:20.193
and he gives his very dramatic lecture.

00:32:22.241 --> 00:32:24.720
It was a very hot summer night in Shanghai

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:26.850
and they were so hot, they
were serving ice cream

00:32:26.850 --> 00:32:28.770
to attract more crowd.

00:32:28.770 --> 00:32:30.690
And Zhou Houkun showed up,

00:32:30.690 --> 00:32:34.533
he had this perfectly
coiffed hair, Western suit.

00:32:37.081 --> 00:32:38.970
I don't think he was a terribly funny man

00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:40.470
from the way he looks.

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:41.943
Pretty serious fellow,

00:32:42.930 --> 00:32:47.930
and he had with him a
briefcase and he carried it up

00:32:47.970 --> 00:32:49.830
to the podium with him,
kinda like where I am.

00:32:49.830 --> 00:32:51.243
He puts it next to his feet.

00:32:52.260 --> 00:32:54.900
And just as everyone quieted down,

00:32:54.900 --> 00:32:56.733
he did this very odd thing.

00:32:57.810 --> 00:33:00.060
He opened up the briefcase

00:33:00.060 --> 00:33:05.060
and took out what looked
like workman's overalls

00:33:06.660 --> 00:33:10.897
and he pulled it over
his suit and he said,

00:33:10.897 --> 00:33:13.197
"Don't be afraid to get your hands dirty."

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:16.830
And what did he mean by that?

00:33:16.830 --> 00:33:20.100
Because traditionally,
manual labor was looked down

00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:21.633
upon in China.

00:33:23.441 --> 00:33:26.280
There's a reason why it's a
society or civilization built

00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:30.450
on literacy and learnedness
and prized learning as such.

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:33.370
So anyone who worked with a
manual labor like craftsmen

00:33:35.010 --> 00:33:37.380
or carpentry, it was really
kinda like secondary.

00:33:37.380 --> 00:33:39.270
It was more like a skill, right?

00:33:39.270 --> 00:33:41.420
It was not knowledge.
It's more like skill.

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:44.880
But Zhou Houkun, having gone
through an engineering program

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:49.230
in the United States, having
actually interned in factories,

00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:51.570
knew that was not the case.

00:33:51.570 --> 00:33:52.560
And to modernize,

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:56.340
China had to strengthen
engineering mechanics

00:33:56.340 --> 00:33:57.990
as scientific learning.

00:33:57.990 --> 00:34:02.550
And to do that, he believed
you had to correct a prejudice

00:34:02.550 --> 00:34:05.163
against the kind of
modernity and modernization.

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:08.280
So it was a very successful night.

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:12.060
Unfortunately, his efforts
didn't go very far.

00:34:12.060 --> 00:34:15.600
He actually got a job at
Commercial Press, Commercial Press,

00:34:15.600 --> 00:34:17.580
the same press we were talking about,

00:34:17.580 --> 00:34:20.670
except he couldn't
really quite pull it off.

00:34:20.670 --> 00:34:21.780
The parts that he required

00:34:21.780 --> 00:34:24.240
for the typewriter was too expensive

00:34:24.240 --> 00:34:27.300
and he was so accustomed to
the conditions at MIT and,

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:30.240
you know, in the United
States that he was appalled

00:34:30.240 --> 00:34:33.090
by the degree of, you
know, workmanship, right,

00:34:33.090 --> 00:34:34.233
precision workmanship.

00:34:35.430 --> 00:34:37.380
He was very frustrated and decided,

00:34:37.380 --> 00:34:38.527
he would tell Commercial Press like,

00:34:38.527 --> 00:34:39.960
"Why don't you gimme money to send me back

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:42.597
to the United States and
let me do this there?"

00:34:43.590 --> 00:34:44.880
And the editor at the time,

00:34:44.880 --> 00:34:49.546
who is this classic Confucian
scholar, very, very shrewd,

00:34:49.546 --> 00:34:50.379
he's like,

00:34:50.379 --> 00:34:51.780
"No, no, no, we're not putting money back

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:53.850
in America's pockets.

00:34:53.850 --> 00:34:55.860
You have to do that here."

00:34:55.860 --> 00:34:56.693
But he said,

00:34:56.693 --> 00:34:59.037
"You know what, why don't you just go?

00:34:59.037 --> 00:35:02.730
You know, you'd be free of our contract,

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:04.320
free fine contract."

00:35:04.320 --> 00:35:07.260
And it's very clever because
what Commercial Press did was,

00:35:07.260 --> 00:35:10.200
they kept the prototype
and they kept the blueprint

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:11.193
of this typewriter.

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:13.860
So Zhou Houkun actually
ended up doing fine.

00:35:13.860 --> 00:35:15.360
You don't have to worry
too much about him,

00:35:15.360 --> 00:35:17.790
but he didn't do much with his typewriter.

00:35:17.790 --> 00:35:19.147
But then Commercial Press decided,

00:35:19.147 --> 00:35:21.298
"You know, these Ivy League returnings."

00:35:21.298 --> 00:35:22.131
(Jing laughing)

00:35:22.131 --> 00:35:24.240
"These Ivy League registered
educator returnees,

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:26.700
they come back and they're
spoiled and, you know,

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:28.170
they expect this and that.

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:30.360
We need someone hungrier."

00:35:30.360 --> 00:35:32.340
So he found this man, Shu,

00:35:32.340 --> 00:35:36.840
a young man who didn't get to
go to America for education

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:39.790
and he came back and he basically
produced this typewriter.

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:44.620
But still, that did not
quite solve all the problems

00:35:46.500 --> 00:35:48.960
because you're still talking
about whole characters.

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:51.480
So we're not exactly using
characters like alphabet, right?

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:53.910
You still can't break it
down like the way you can,

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:55.470
a word into letters.

00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:58.320
So you still have to deal
with the sheer challenge

00:35:58.320 --> 00:35:59.793
of number and size.

00:36:01.080 --> 00:36:04.590
So amidst all these people,

00:36:04.590 --> 00:36:08.550
all these inventors and you
know, mavericks and eccentrics,

00:36:08.550 --> 00:36:11.790
because there were many
eccentrics in this race,

00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:16.790
there was another one that came
to rise above everyone else.

00:36:17.190 --> 00:36:19.983
That is, I'm happy to say,
my writer, Lin Yutang.

00:36:23.310 --> 00:36:27.360
Already, we see a man who I think is born

00:36:27.360 --> 00:36:30.873
to be well bred and well liked.

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:36.330
And he was actually a famous
humorist. He wrote essays.

00:36:36.330 --> 00:36:39.180
He became, actually, the most
beloved and probably still

00:36:39.180 --> 00:36:43.770
to date the most well known
Chinese writer in America.

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:45.603
He was here in the 30s and 40s.

00:36:46.680 --> 00:36:49.140
So Lin Yutang came up
with the idea of a clear

00:36:49.140 --> 00:36:51.180
and quick typewriter.

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:52.680
And why is it clear and quick?

00:36:53.670 --> 00:36:56.280
He went back and looked
at the Chinese character

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:58.410
and realized that, remember,

00:36:58.410 --> 00:37:00.870
I told you just a few minutes
ago that Chinese characters,

00:37:00.870 --> 00:37:02.760
you can use one part,

00:37:02.760 --> 00:37:05.340
it's actually called
the classifier radical

00:37:05.340 --> 00:37:08.370
to identify into group characters.

00:37:08.370 --> 00:37:09.203
He had this idea,

00:37:09.203 --> 00:37:13.020
"Well, why can't you treat
all parts of a character as

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:14.940
though it were radical?"

00:37:14.940 --> 00:37:19.080
So you basically break down
each character into parts

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:20.640
or what he called components,

00:37:20.640 --> 00:37:22.443
what came to be known as components.

00:37:23.400 --> 00:37:26.430
And once you have these
components, you can lay them out,

00:37:26.430 --> 00:37:28.710
map 'em onto an alphabetic
keyboard, right,

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:30.610
like the QWERTY keyboard we still use,

00:37:31.560 --> 00:37:34.350
and then you can use that
as a way of combining

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:36.093
and recombining characters.

00:37:36.990 --> 00:37:41.250
So this is actually quite
brilliant because of that turn,

00:37:41.250 --> 00:37:44.460
that shift in mentality, right?

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:47.820
Lin Yutang managed to basically
map Chinese characters

00:37:47.820 --> 00:37:49.023
onto this keyboard.

00:37:50.070 --> 00:37:53.850
And what you have is, I think
the top two rows, top two,

00:37:53.850 --> 00:37:56.130
three rows has the top components.

00:37:56.130 --> 00:37:57.420
He had this idea.

00:37:57.420 --> 00:37:59.190
Even if you don't know characters at all,

00:37:59.190 --> 00:38:01.020
if you look at like a character,

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:04.950
surely you can tell what's
on top and what's on bottom.

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:06.480
That's actually all you need

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:09.240
because he broke out these
parts into top and bottoms

00:38:09.240 --> 00:38:10.560
on the keyboard

00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:14.340
so that you select a top
key, you select a bottom key,

00:38:14.340 --> 00:38:16.473
you press both keys together.

00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:21.150
Okay, it's still a few more
steps, but bear with me.

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:23.790
But then you have this,
what's called a magic viewer.

00:38:23.790 --> 00:38:24.930
It's like a little window,

00:38:24.930 --> 00:38:29.130
a physical window that pops up and just

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:33.720
through his statistical analysis,
it will give you anywhere

00:38:33.720 --> 00:38:37.773
between five to eight Chinese
characters that fit the bill.

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:42.540
And then you visually identify
which one of those five

00:38:42.540 --> 00:38:45.330
or eight is the character you have in mind

00:38:45.330 --> 00:38:47.310
and then you press a key,

00:38:47.310 --> 00:38:51.019
which is actually where
the current space bar is

00:38:51.019 --> 00:38:52.290
where you have your keyboard space bar,

00:38:52.290 --> 00:38:54.360
he divided that up into eight keys,

00:38:54.360 --> 00:38:55.800
number one through eight.

00:38:55.800 --> 00:38:58.650
So you just press the corresponding key.

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:01.290
So in three steps, you can
put the character you want

00:39:01.290 --> 00:39:02.373
on a piece of paper.

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:05.550
It's actually quite
extraordinary 'cause also,

00:39:05.550 --> 00:39:09.090
this is still mechanical age.

00:39:09.090 --> 00:39:10.920
This is like, far before electronic,

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:13.680
let alone mechanical or let alone digital.

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:16.290
So this was all done with levers and poles

00:39:16.290 --> 00:39:18.840
and maximizing what's
inside the wheels and axles.

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:20.240
It was pretty extraordinary,

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:23.700
and this is what that keyboard looks like.

00:39:26.083 --> 00:39:27.750
This is actually from
the patent application

00:39:27.750 --> 00:39:30.540
that he submitted in the
late 1940s that was approved,

00:39:30.540 --> 00:39:32.163
I believe, in 1952.

00:39:34.530 --> 00:39:39.090
Now, he gives demonstrations.

00:39:39.090 --> 00:39:42.870
Again, you can see he's very
camera friendly, actually.

00:39:42.870 --> 00:39:44.730
And that's his daughter, by the way,

00:39:44.730 --> 00:39:47.880
who will go to do these demos with him.

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:50.040
And essentially, he
said the magic viewers,

00:39:50.040 --> 00:39:52.710
the one that you see works
or opens out to the window,

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:54.060
it's kinda this cumbersome thing

00:39:54.060 --> 00:39:57.630
but if any of you that might
use Chinese characters,

00:39:57.630 --> 00:40:00.930
when you type, you will
also notice that these days,

00:40:00.930 --> 00:40:02.450
when you type in what you want,

00:40:02.450 --> 00:40:04.620
if you spell out the Chinese character,

00:40:04.620 --> 00:40:07.020
you also get a window, right?

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:10.410
You still can't just fish
out the exact one you want.

00:40:10.410 --> 00:40:13.290
You get a window of the likely candidates

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:14.123
and you basically have to like,

00:40:14.123 --> 00:40:16.830
scroll through, scroll
through, scroll through.

00:40:16.830 --> 00:40:19.863
That repeatedly comes from
Lin Yutang's invention.

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:23.160
But there's an even bigger deal with this,

00:40:23.160 --> 00:40:26.010
a kind of backstory and
this is how, you know,

00:40:26.010 --> 00:40:29.010
these different histories
have a way of connecting

00:40:29.010 --> 00:40:33.153
and getting forgotten and
then remembered again.

00:40:34.980 --> 00:40:38.520
Now, Lin Yutang also had
a similar disappointment

00:40:38.520 --> 00:40:42.450
with his typewriter because of
the problem of mass scaling.

00:40:42.450 --> 00:40:44.850
So that's been the bottleneck all along.

00:40:44.850 --> 00:40:48.150
You can create a great prototype,
but this comes to, okay,

00:40:48.150 --> 00:40:51.000
like, how do we build thousands, hundreds,

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:52.353
not just one or two.

00:40:53.809 --> 00:40:55.680
And he couldn't find
factories that could do this

00:40:55.680 --> 00:40:57.840
and the parts, because they
all had to be customized,

00:40:57.840 --> 00:40:59.820
was very expensive.

00:40:59.820 --> 00:41:01.950
So he eventually sold it

00:41:01.950 --> 00:41:04.300
to the Mergenthaler Linotype company

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:10.650
and they gave him 25,000
dollars for it, okay?

00:41:10.650 --> 00:41:13.620
But this was also the 50s.

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:17.970
This was the era of
Cold War, cryptography,

00:41:17.970 --> 00:41:21.390
where machine translation,
where the United States

00:41:21.390 --> 00:41:24.180
and the Americans, Russians
were trying to break into

00:41:24.180 --> 00:41:26.220
and intercept each other's messages or,

00:41:26.220 --> 00:41:28.530
you know, using machine
to translate, you know,

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:30.270
automatic translation.

00:41:30.270 --> 00:41:34.053
And the Chinese language figured
a prominent role in this.

00:41:35.310 --> 00:41:38.220
So Mergenthaler handed it over

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:42.120
to actually the United States
Air Force who then handed it

00:41:42.120 --> 00:41:45.363
over to IBM for further development.

00:41:46.320 --> 00:41:48.870
In the summer of 1967, I believe,

00:41:48.870 --> 00:41:50.920
I'll have to check that, maybe it was 63,

00:41:52.050 --> 00:41:55.863
IBM unveiled something
called the sinal writer.

00:41:58.638 --> 00:41:59.760
It had a couple of different names,

00:41:59.760 --> 00:42:03.660
but the sinal writer basically
used Lin Yutang's keyboard,

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:05.100
the one you just saw,

00:42:05.100 --> 00:42:09.270
this one to create basically
a prototype that will lead

00:42:09.270 --> 00:42:12.363
to later, Chinese computing
in the United States.

00:42:13.470 --> 00:42:15.870
Now, this was not the only path

00:42:15.870 --> 00:42:19.110
because there was also a
project at MIT at the time

00:42:19.110 --> 00:42:23.313
with this graphic arts
research foundation, GARF,

00:42:24.150 --> 00:42:26.310
based in Cambridge that was also trying

00:42:26.310 --> 00:42:29.850
to build kind of first
Chinese computer, so to speak.

00:42:29.850 --> 00:42:34.110
But they too, this man Samuel
Caldwell, who people thought,

00:42:34.110 --> 00:42:37.350
you know, I think he's
been called the father

00:42:37.350 --> 00:42:38.460
of Chinese computing.

00:42:38.460 --> 00:42:41.490
He was an American. He
didn't know Chinese.

00:42:41.490 --> 00:42:44.100
And what's also not very well known is

00:42:44.100 --> 00:42:49.100
that he also relied on Lin
Yutang's character analysis

00:42:50.250 --> 00:42:54.480
because when he was
first called in to talk

00:42:54.480 --> 00:42:57.990
about the project, I think
there was three people

00:42:57.990 --> 00:42:58.823
in the room.

00:42:58.823 --> 00:43:00.393
There was him, his boss,

00:43:01.230 --> 00:43:05.640
a third person who was a
Chinese graduate student, PhD,

00:43:06.780 --> 00:43:09.210
who came to work with Caldwell.

00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:12.600
And he was handed a task
of doing a first survey

00:43:12.600 --> 00:43:14.940
into the feasibility of
doing something like this

00:43:14.940 --> 00:43:17.700
and he basically provided all
the Chinese language know-how

00:43:17.700 --> 00:43:18.533
to Caldwell.

00:43:20.100 --> 00:43:23.010
So, one of the reasons
I really like about,

00:43:23.010 --> 00:43:25.950
this is one of the many
stories that interweave

00:43:25.950 --> 00:43:28.260
through "Kingdom of Characters",

00:43:28.260 --> 00:43:33.090
is really how something
so niche and so esoteric

00:43:33.090 --> 00:43:36.960
and so overlooked could
play such a big role

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:40.620
into the globalization and the possibility

00:43:40.620 --> 00:43:43.050
of China's presence in the world now.

00:43:43.050 --> 00:43:46.710
Because getting Chinese
into computing was the door

00:43:46.710 --> 00:43:48.630
that would open all other doors.

00:43:48.630 --> 00:43:50.820
And even during cultural revolution,

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:54.210
the state very much
recognized that and they had

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:57.333
to grit their teeth through obviously,

00:43:58.590 --> 00:44:01.200
I think, the extreme conditions
of culture revolution

00:44:01.200 --> 00:44:03.570
but even throughout
the culture revolution,

00:44:03.570 --> 00:44:06.900
there was always
individuals like Lin Yutang,

00:44:06.900 --> 00:44:10.080
like Zhou Houkun who
kept that threat going.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:13.050
And to me, the Chinese Script Revolution,

00:44:13.050 --> 00:44:15.540
was the true people's revolution, right?

00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:17.790
And it's the longest, most forgotten,

00:44:17.790 --> 00:44:21.090
and it's not the people
as in what is invoked

00:44:21.090 --> 00:44:22.590
by the Chinese government.

00:44:22.590 --> 00:44:25.470
It is not the people invoked
by the nationalist government

00:44:25.470 --> 00:44:26.370
before that.

00:44:26.370 --> 00:44:29.280
It's really people from all walks of life,

00:44:29.280 --> 00:44:30.420
you know, the second,

00:44:30.420 --> 00:44:33.690
third stringers of history, I like to say,

00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:37.380
who are neither radical
nor really revolutionary,

00:44:37.380 --> 00:44:40.290
but they were the ones
who picked up the pieces

00:44:40.290 --> 00:44:43.350
after every war, after every invasion,

00:44:43.350 --> 00:44:46.230
and to think about they're
the ones who cared and think

00:44:46.230 --> 00:44:49.740
about what China is
gonna be in the future.

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:53.731
So I leave that story here and
I'll welcome any questions.

00:44:53.731 --> 00:44:56.731
(audience clapping)

00:45:02.010 --> 00:45:03.150
- Thank you so much, Jing,

00:45:03.150 --> 00:45:05.730
for that absolutely fascinating talk.

00:45:05.730 --> 00:45:10.335
We have a microphone that is moving.

00:45:10.335 --> 00:45:13.335
(microphone moving)

00:45:25.230 --> 00:45:27.322
For the young volunteer who relishes.

00:45:27.322 --> 00:45:29.167
- Oh my goodness.

00:45:29.167 --> 00:45:32.000
(people laughing)

00:45:37.123 --> 00:45:38.241
- What do I do?

00:45:38.241 --> 00:45:39.074
(everyone laughing)

00:45:39.074 --> 00:45:39.991
- Just talk.

00:45:39.991 --> 00:45:41.664
- Is that, oh, good.

00:45:41.664 --> 00:45:43.530
(everyone laughing)

00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:44.760
So I just wanna clarify,

00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:49.760
is Lin Yutang derived radicals
or recognized radical?

00:45:50.220 --> 00:45:54.270
He was the first person
to recognize radicals?

00:45:54.270 --> 00:45:59.250
- He did it in a particular
way in an essay in 1917.

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:01.170
So he was a young man.

00:46:01.170 --> 00:46:04.590
No one really knew exactly
who he was, really.

00:46:04.590 --> 00:46:06.990
He wrote these two very
un-sexy essays for the time

00:46:06.990 --> 00:46:09.060
'cause everybody's talking
about revolution and, you know,

00:46:09.060 --> 00:46:11.880
vernacularization and
overthrowing this and that.

00:46:11.880 --> 00:46:15.810
He wrote a very modest piece
about library catalogs,

00:46:15.810 --> 00:46:17.520
library index system.

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:18.900
And then after that,

00:46:18.900 --> 00:46:22.470
he actually was not part of
this race in the 20s and 30s

00:46:22.470 --> 00:46:24.330
because then, he went abroad.

00:46:24.330 --> 00:46:27.180
He went and got his PhD
in Leipzig, Germany.

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:30.450
He went to Harvard, he became a writer.

00:46:30.450 --> 00:46:34.560
But that opening salvo,
everybody else heard it.

00:46:34.560 --> 00:46:37.710
And so, a lot of the inventors
in the book were the ones

00:46:37.710 --> 00:46:39.390
that kind of followed his footsteps,

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:41.160
but then did something new with it.

00:46:41.160 --> 00:46:43.053
- Hmm. Hm, thank you.

00:46:45.870 --> 00:46:46.912
Can I throw this?

00:46:46.912 --> 00:46:47.745
- I think that's what I'm worried about.

00:46:47.745 --> 00:46:50.462
- I think you just toss it. (laughs)

00:46:50.462 --> 00:46:52.353
- Okay, next question?

00:46:55.350 --> 00:46:57.630
I'm gonna toss it. Shall I toss it?

00:46:57.630 --> 00:47:00.420
- Oh my goodness. (laughs)

00:47:00.420 --> 00:47:01.903
- Yes. Okay.

00:47:01.903 --> 00:47:06.903
So, all of that took place
before simplified characters?

00:47:10.050 --> 00:47:11.793
- I'm so glad you asked that.

00:47:13.350 --> 00:47:17.520
You know, we think of simplified writing,

00:47:17.520 --> 00:47:21.390
simplified characters and
also romanization pinging

00:47:21.390 --> 00:47:25.920
as the twin achievements
under Mao in the 50s.

00:47:25.920 --> 00:47:27.690
But you'd be surprised to know

00:47:27.690 --> 00:47:30.630
that simplified characters
were proposed way before them

00:47:30.630 --> 00:47:35.250
by the nationalists in as early as 1909.

00:47:35.250 --> 00:47:36.930
And in the 30s,

00:47:36.930 --> 00:47:39.090
the nationalists were
the ones who proposed,

00:47:39.090 --> 00:47:40.800
while they were the
reigning government then,

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:43.890
they proposed a set of
characters to be simplified,

00:47:43.890 --> 00:47:47.130
but they couldn't push it
through for two reasons.

00:47:47.130 --> 00:47:49.773
China was in the throes of war, right?

00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:52.290
Throughout the 20th century,

00:47:52.290 --> 00:47:57.290
it was just torn by internal
like, civil war, war lordism,

00:47:57.450 --> 00:48:01.110
revolution, foreign invasion,
one after the other.

00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:06.110
And the other reason is
because they met resistance.

00:48:08.130 --> 00:48:09.180
There were these, you know,

00:48:09.180 --> 00:48:10.993
older members of the party who actually,

00:48:10.993 --> 00:48:13.560
a very dramatic description of
one scene where one got down

00:48:13.560 --> 00:48:15.277
on his knees to tell him,

00:48:15.277 --> 00:48:16.860
"Leave the Chinese characters alone!"

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:18.930
You know, for them, I mean,

00:48:18.930 --> 00:48:21.960
part of it is a difficulty
in shifting frame of mind,

00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:23.370
like a way of thinking

00:48:23.370 --> 00:48:25.590
and they're just saying
you cannot touch anything

00:48:25.590 --> 00:48:27.570
on the character, like the idea,

00:48:27.570 --> 00:48:30.840
the idea of deforming
Chinese writing system,

00:48:30.840 --> 00:48:35.370
which is obnoxious to
many and unacceptable

00:48:35.370 --> 00:48:36.600
and also because historically,

00:48:36.600 --> 00:48:40.140
Chinese writing has always
been equated with authority.

00:48:40.140 --> 00:48:43.260
There's very few writing systems
that comes with such a rich

00:48:43.260 --> 00:48:47.280
and kind of imposing philosophical
theory about, you know,

00:48:47.280 --> 00:48:48.730
its origin and what it meant.

00:48:50.310 --> 00:48:51.143
- Thank you.

00:48:56.744 --> 00:48:58.353
Pass? (chuckles) All right.

00:49:01.620 --> 00:49:03.720
- [Attendant] Thanks so much.

00:49:03.720 --> 00:49:05.550
I guess my question is building off that.

00:49:05.550 --> 00:49:07.560
Why the 20th century?

00:49:07.560 --> 00:49:10.020
Like, you talk a lot
about like, Hu Shi, right?

00:49:10.020 --> 00:49:12.930
And like, obviously a lot of
early 20th century thinkers

00:49:12.930 --> 00:49:15.840
and like, their drive to kind
of categorize and like, look

00:49:15.840 --> 00:49:17.700
into library science
and there's this like,

00:49:17.700 --> 00:49:21.213
focus on the character
and breaking it down.

00:49:22.920 --> 00:49:25.890
And, you know, what I feel
like a lot of people end

00:49:25.890 --> 00:49:27.570
up learning in Chinese class is like, oh,

00:49:27.570 --> 00:49:31.080
one of the big like,
inventions of the first emperor

00:49:31.080 --> 00:49:33.240
of China is this character system

00:49:33.240 --> 00:49:37.530
to unify all these ethnic
groups and diverse cultures.

00:49:37.530 --> 00:49:41.880
So I'm kind of curious on
why this drive to like,

00:49:41.880 --> 00:49:45.270
in this hyper focus on library
science and categorization

00:49:45.270 --> 00:49:49.320
in the early 20th century
rather than like, earlier.

00:49:49.320 --> 00:49:52.500
- Well this is why this is
the main argument of the book,

00:49:52.500 --> 00:49:54.750
which is because what we saw was,

00:49:54.750 --> 00:49:57.183
when language became infrastructure.

00:49:58.980 --> 00:50:01.110
Today I gave a story
about Chinese typewriting

00:50:01.110 --> 00:50:03.120
and Lin Yutang because he's a writer

00:50:03.120 --> 00:50:04.620
and I'm a literature person,

00:50:04.620 --> 00:50:06.450
but a big part of the book's also

00:50:06.450 --> 00:50:08.850
about telegraphs and telegraphy.

00:50:08.850 --> 00:50:12.870
How do you expect to send
a telegram in Chinese

00:50:12.870 --> 00:50:14.670
if you're using Morse code,

00:50:14.670 --> 00:50:17.550
which is dots and dashes for letters

00:50:17.550 --> 00:50:21.720
and actually numbers too, but
letters not character writing?

00:50:21.720 --> 00:50:23.850
So far in 1870s,

00:50:23.850 --> 00:50:25.830
China went through this
extraordinary period

00:50:25.830 --> 00:50:29.130
of trying to figure
out how to enter itself

00:50:29.130 --> 00:50:31.710
into an air of telegraphy
and at that time,

00:50:31.710 --> 00:50:34.170
it couldn't take control of its fate

00:50:34.170 --> 00:50:36.090
because there was all this competition

00:50:36.090 --> 00:50:38.610
between actually the Danes
and the British to break it.

00:50:38.610 --> 00:50:40.800
They had the big telegraphic
companies, an interest

00:50:40.800 --> 00:50:42.900
in breaking into the far East.

00:50:42.900 --> 00:50:46.110
And so the Danes, there's
this very famous episode,

00:50:46.110 --> 00:50:48.420
one moonless night I talk about this

00:50:48.420 --> 00:50:50.790
where they basically
secretly snuck onto cables

00:50:50.790 --> 00:50:53.640
onto Chinese territory and just built it,

00:50:53.640 --> 00:50:54.877
despite the Chinese having said,

00:50:54.877 --> 00:50:56.160
"No, don't do it."

00:50:56.160 --> 00:50:57.990
And there's a lot of popular revolt, too,

00:50:57.990 --> 00:51:00.810
against foreigners building
telegraphs because of course,

00:51:00.810 --> 00:51:03.300
some of these cables go underground.

00:51:03.300 --> 00:51:04.470
And of course for Chinese,

00:51:04.470 --> 00:51:07.380
this is blasphemous
because they were digging

00:51:07.380 --> 00:51:12.380
through ancestral graves and
the idea that you would disturb

00:51:12.510 --> 00:51:17.510
and basically, you know,
defame your ancestors

00:51:18.690 --> 00:51:22.140
that way was just utterly unacceptable.

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:24.480
So there were popular
revolts on countryside

00:51:24.480 --> 00:51:25.650
against foreign intrusion

00:51:25.650 --> 00:51:28.110
so telegraphy became a
part of foreign intrusion.

00:51:28.110 --> 00:51:30.660
But all this really is to say it started,

00:51:30.660 --> 00:51:32.910
we can trace this to opium war, right?

00:51:32.910 --> 00:51:35.040
That forced China to try to figure

00:51:35.040 --> 00:51:37.830
out what its standing
was, vis-a-vis the West.

00:51:37.830 --> 00:51:38.820
But more importantly, you know,

00:51:38.820 --> 00:51:41.730
telegraphy, typewriting, computing,

00:51:41.730 --> 00:51:45.720
these were all critical
bottlenecks in the history

00:51:45.720 --> 00:51:48.810
of what's become the most
important thing of our day,

00:51:48.810 --> 00:51:51.900
which is global communications technology.

00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:53.760
So China, at each step of the way,

00:51:53.760 --> 00:51:56.730
it was trying to break into
this infrastructure, right?

00:51:56.730 --> 00:51:59.850
But it had to mold itself
to an alphabetic structure

00:51:59.850 --> 00:52:03.420
because the Western alphabet
had the first mover advantage.

00:52:03.420 --> 00:52:06.780
And in the history of
technology, you know,

00:52:06.780 --> 00:52:07.950
technology and science,

00:52:07.950 --> 00:52:10.980
you all learn very quickly
that it's almost impossible

00:52:10.980 --> 00:52:13.470
to break a first mover's advantage

00:52:13.470 --> 00:52:15.873
because a class example was QWERTY.

00:52:16.860 --> 00:52:18.960
I mean I think most people
know about the QWERTY keyboard,

00:52:18.960 --> 00:52:20.070
where it comes from.

00:52:20.070 --> 00:52:22.593
Why is it, you know, it's
ordered that, with the letters

00:52:22.593 --> 00:52:23.426
that are put next to each other?

00:52:23.426 --> 00:52:25.740
So to prevent hammers from sticking,

00:52:25.740 --> 00:52:27.840
we use these really old style typewriters,

00:52:27.840 --> 00:52:30.420
which I remember my grandmother
had when I was a kid.

00:52:30.420 --> 00:52:32.910
So, you know, that's
completely useless now.

00:52:32.910 --> 00:52:33.780
They're using computers.

00:52:33.780 --> 00:52:35.370
We don't use those clunky typewriters

00:52:35.370 --> 00:52:39.390
and we haven't in a long time,
but that habit is ingrained.

00:52:39.390 --> 00:52:42.390
So even though other more
accessible and, you know,

00:52:42.390 --> 00:52:45.300
better keyboards came
along the Dvorak thing,

00:52:45.300 --> 00:52:48.360
it didn't really matter
because people were habituated.

00:52:48.360 --> 00:52:51.720
So you can imagine how after
thousands and thousands

00:52:51.720 --> 00:52:55.200
of years of using a
particular writing system

00:52:55.200 --> 00:52:59.070
and to shift to figure
out, how do we communicate,

00:52:59.070 --> 00:53:01.320
resistance to communicate
with the alphabet,

00:53:01.320 --> 00:53:04.320
it took extraordinary determination,

00:53:04.320 --> 00:53:09.320
but also I think change,
the willingness to change,

00:53:09.810 --> 00:53:12.990
which was very, very difficult
for China to do, right?

00:53:12.990 --> 00:53:15.060
If you imagine an empire on its last legs

00:53:15.060 --> 00:53:17.520
in late 19th century, all of a sudden,

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:18.780
it's this brave new world.

00:53:18.780 --> 00:53:20.850
They had learned something
called technology.

00:53:20.850 --> 00:53:22.213
That's why they were
just sending students,

00:53:22.213 --> 00:53:23.340
like Zhou Houkun abroad

00:53:23.340 --> 00:53:26.820
'cause they didn't have
the technical know-how.

00:53:26.820 --> 00:53:28.890
So that's what unleashed all this.

00:53:28.890 --> 00:53:30.660
So we think, I mean,

00:53:30.660 --> 00:53:33.000
little did I know that when
I started this project,

00:53:33.000 --> 00:53:35.910
that library of science would
be the one thing I spent a lot

00:53:35.910 --> 00:53:38.250
of time with or that my favorite character

00:53:38.250 --> 00:53:40.440
in the book is actually the librarian

00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:44.070
in chapter four who called
himself Bismarck Deux

00:53:44.070 --> 00:53:46.440
because he wanted to rule
the field of library science

00:53:46.440 --> 00:53:49.320
with an iron fist. (laughs)

00:53:49.320 --> 00:53:52.050
So there's, you know, these
extraordinary stories,

00:53:52.050 --> 00:53:55.170
I think in the individuals
'cause it's not often the state,

00:53:55.170 --> 00:53:56.880
that the Chinese nation who's competing.

00:53:56.880 --> 00:53:58.020
It's wasn't like that.

00:53:58.020 --> 00:54:01.140
China was in such disarray, you know,

00:54:01.140 --> 00:54:02.820
throughout the 20th century.

00:54:02.820 --> 00:54:06.873
So much loss of lives,
so much displacement.

00:54:08.190 --> 00:54:10.320
That's why it's all the
more remarkable that

00:54:10.320 --> 00:54:14.973
in those dark times, and
there's some very dark times,

00:54:16.770 --> 00:54:18.450
what inspired my innovators

00:54:18.450 --> 00:54:21.870
and these individuals is precisely that.

00:54:21.870 --> 00:54:23.490
The light at the end of the tunnel,

00:54:23.490 --> 00:54:25.110
it was something they could hold onto,

00:54:25.110 --> 00:54:26.670
something they could save, right?

00:54:26.670 --> 00:54:29.640
Something they possessed
because it was language

00:54:29.640 --> 00:54:33.003
and they felt that if
they do what they could,

00:54:34.140 --> 00:54:36.993
they may help one day to
contribute to China's rise.

00:54:38.790 --> 00:54:41.310
- [Priya] I think as you note,
right, it's very interesting

00:54:41.310 --> 00:54:44.880
that library science is really
how Google got its start.

00:54:44.880 --> 00:54:49.170
Their first proposal to NSF
was a library classification

00:54:49.170 --> 00:54:51.450
and the search engine
really came out of that.

00:54:51.450 --> 00:54:52.283
- Yeah.

00:54:52.283 --> 00:54:55.290
- So it's kind of interesting
how all these loops go back

00:54:55.290 --> 00:54:58.890
and forth to classification
of information.

00:54:58.890 --> 00:55:01.530
It really rules the
world, right? Even now.

00:55:01.530 --> 00:55:04.080
- And knowledge becomes so
important in our times, right?

00:55:04.080 --> 00:55:05.370
Think about Wikipedia.

00:55:05.370 --> 00:55:06.203
- Right.

00:55:06.203 --> 00:55:07.740
- And how it acts like a knowledge base,

00:55:07.740 --> 00:55:09.633
the idea of linking knowledge base.

00:55:11.610 --> 00:55:14.190
If China did not make
that step and get itself

00:55:14.190 --> 00:55:18.720
in a digital age, none of what
it is now would be possible

00:55:18.720 --> 00:55:19.890
at all.

00:55:19.890 --> 00:55:21.348
Yep.

00:55:21.348 --> 00:55:24.210
(Priya speaking indistinctly)

00:55:24.210 --> 00:55:25.920
- [Attendant 2] Hi,
thank you for this talk.

00:55:25.920 --> 00:55:27.690
I was really fascinated

00:55:27.690 --> 00:55:29.910
by the Lin Yutang's typewriter method

00:55:29.910 --> 00:55:31.650
when you brought it up and then I went

00:55:31.650 --> 00:55:32.490
through this process of thought.

00:55:32.490 --> 00:55:33.323
I was like,

00:55:33.323 --> 00:55:34.590
"Oh yeah, I know what that is.

00:55:34.590 --> 00:55:36.780
Wait a minute. I don't
know how to type Chinese.

00:55:36.780 --> 00:55:38.040
I don't know what that is."

00:55:38.040 --> 00:55:40.200
'Cause I used that in Korean and Japanese,

00:55:40.200 --> 00:55:41.400
but there's a different approach.

00:55:41.400 --> 00:55:43.380
Of course, you type it
out phonetically first

00:55:43.380 --> 00:55:45.360
and you don't have to like,

00:55:45.360 --> 00:55:46.680
but you still use Chinese characters.

00:55:46.680 --> 00:55:47.910
- Well now we use, right,

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:50.130
now we use basically alphabet letters.

00:55:50.130 --> 00:55:50.963
- [Attendant 2] Yeah.

00:55:50.963 --> 00:55:51.796
In Chinese, yeah. I've heard
about that in China anyway.

00:55:51.796 --> 00:55:54.450
- But in China like, you
still see these keyboards

00:55:54.450 --> 00:55:55.410
because think of it this way.

00:55:55.410 --> 00:55:59.190
If you can break down Chinese
into what turns out to be two

00:55:59.190 --> 00:56:01.470
to four components,

00:56:01.470 --> 00:56:04.140
every character can be
broken down to either like,

00:56:04.140 --> 00:56:06.870
they get from anywhere from
two to four components.

00:56:06.870 --> 00:56:11.870
That means two to four
keystrokes to make a character.

00:56:12.960 --> 00:56:14.220
There are a lot of English words

00:56:14.220 --> 00:56:16.410
that are not just four letters, right?

00:56:16.410 --> 00:56:19.860
So in some ways you
can type faster, right?

00:56:19.860 --> 00:56:22.410
Typing Chinese faster
on alphabetic keyboard.

00:56:22.410 --> 00:56:24.300
So it's this remarkable, you know,

00:56:24.300 --> 00:56:27.690
outcome of being able to
harness alphabet technology

00:56:27.690 --> 00:56:29.640
and make Chinese better, right?

00:56:29.640 --> 00:56:32.250
So instead of just becoming
completely wholesale,

00:56:32.250 --> 00:56:34.590
alphabetized, which China could have,

00:56:34.590 --> 00:56:37.350
it could just go with the
romanization system we have now.

00:56:37.350 --> 00:56:40.740
But it's precisely because of
the will, passion, devotion

00:56:40.740 --> 00:56:45.740
of people that I talk about
that character writing lived on.

00:56:45.840 --> 00:56:46.680
- [Attendant 2] Right,
I was wondering, though,

00:56:46.680 --> 00:56:48.330
with Korean and Japanese,

00:56:48.330 --> 00:56:50.340
I don't know how familiar
you are with like,

00:56:50.340 --> 00:56:51.340
the history of this,

00:56:52.221 --> 00:56:54.330
'cause they had their
own challenges of course

00:56:54.330 --> 00:56:56.760
to making up mechanical

00:56:56.760 --> 00:56:58.620
and then computerized
type writing differently

00:56:58.620 --> 00:57:00.780
than writing in other languages.

00:57:00.780 --> 00:57:04.380
But do you know if they like,
all just happened to converge

00:57:04.380 --> 00:57:06.480
on that system where like,
'cause when I typed in Korean,

00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:07.410
I type it up phonetically.

00:57:07.410 --> 00:57:10.170
If I wanna make a hanja,
a Chinese character,

00:57:10.170 --> 00:57:13.470
it will be a numbered list
and I can like, hit the number

00:57:13.470 --> 00:57:16.410
and get the one I want to sort of turn

00:57:16.410 --> 00:57:17.700
that phonetic character,

00:57:17.700 --> 00:57:19.530
phonetic block into a Chinese character

00:57:19.530 --> 00:57:20.850
and similar in Japanese.

00:57:20.850 --> 00:57:23.280
Do you know if this is all
is like they were all looking

00:57:23.280 --> 00:57:26.220
to China and this all linearly traces back

00:57:26.220 --> 00:57:29.520
to Lin Yutang or it was
just convergent evolution

00:57:29.520 --> 00:57:33.720
on like, what worked best, like
some more like miraculously?

00:57:33.720 --> 00:57:35.700
- I'm glad you asked that
question because sometimes,

00:57:35.700 --> 00:57:37.410
I get like, you know, grumbles

00:57:37.410 --> 00:57:40.644
from Japan knowledges who say
I don't give enough to Japan

00:57:40.644 --> 00:57:43.830
because Japan, of course, have
been using Chinese characters

00:57:43.830 --> 00:57:45.810
as part of their three writing systems.

00:57:45.810 --> 00:57:48.810
And in fact, early Japanese
typewriters were also used

00:57:48.810 --> 00:57:50.010
as references.

00:57:50.010 --> 00:57:51.420
But there's one critical difference,

00:57:51.420 --> 00:57:53.610
which China will make this
argument again and again

00:57:53.610 --> 00:57:55.020
throughout the 20th century.

00:57:55.020 --> 00:57:58.050
Japan only has a relatively
small number of characters

00:57:58.050 --> 00:57:59.250
that it uses.

00:57:59.250 --> 00:58:01.110
China only uses characters.

00:58:01.110 --> 00:58:03.420
So, you know, it's like
this classic, you know,

00:58:03.420 --> 00:58:04.320
this classic saying.

00:58:04.320 --> 00:58:07.740
We talk about China, size matters, right?

00:58:07.740 --> 00:58:11.460
So can it scale up technology
in Japan for its own use?

00:58:11.460 --> 00:58:12.293
Not quite.

00:58:12.293 --> 00:58:13.710
It needed something more expedient,

00:58:13.710 --> 00:58:17.910
more encompassing that's
also more affordable, right?

00:58:17.910 --> 00:58:18.960
But I'm glad you brought that up

00:58:18.960 --> 00:58:20.850
'cause there's definitely
a lot of, you know,

00:58:20.850 --> 00:58:23.940
like, inter-regional quibbles
that I won't talk about now,

00:58:23.940 --> 00:58:25.860
but they are as good as that challenger,

00:58:25.860 --> 00:58:27.810
that young challenger I told you about.

00:58:30.728 --> 00:58:32.528
- Where does the microphone go next?

00:58:35.043 --> 00:58:36.417
- [Attendant 2] I don't think
I can throw it that far.

00:58:37.614 --> 00:58:39.764
- You can throw it
towards an intermediary.

00:58:42.750 --> 00:58:44.587
- [Attendant 3] How's it going? There?

00:58:46.980 --> 00:58:47.813
There you go.

00:58:50.340 --> 00:58:51.173
- [Attendant 4] Thank you.

00:58:51.173 --> 00:58:53.280
I was really interested
when you mentioned the like,

00:58:53.280 --> 00:58:56.520
neuroscientific differences
between phonetic languages

00:58:56.520 --> 00:59:00.150
or not, like, alphabet
languages and geographic ones.

00:59:00.150 --> 00:59:01.770
And I was wondering with pinging

00:59:01.770 --> 00:59:03.270
and like more phoneticization

00:59:03.270 --> 00:59:05.610
when you do like character
input and typing,

00:59:05.610 --> 00:59:07.197
does that change the
way people learn Chinese

00:59:07.197 --> 00:59:10.260
and the way it's like,
represented in the mind?

00:59:10.260 --> 00:59:11.760
- That's a very good question.

00:59:11.760 --> 00:59:13.920
I would have to refer
you to the neuroscientist

00:59:13.920 --> 00:59:16.800
to whom may pursue this at greater depth.

00:59:16.800 --> 00:59:19.710
I mean, but what you point
out is something very valid is

00:59:19.710 --> 00:59:24.513
that we interact with writing
a language differently now.

00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:29.610
I think we're very close to
bypassing keyboard, right?

00:59:29.610 --> 00:59:31.710
Because it's so much about voice input,

00:59:31.710 --> 00:59:35.520
activation and AI technology
is really gonna change a lot

00:59:35.520 --> 00:59:36.390
of that.

00:59:36.390 --> 00:59:38.430
So, you know, one might also ask whether,

00:59:38.430 --> 00:59:41.070
you know, this entire century, right,

00:59:41.070 --> 00:59:43.530
or actually 400 years,
of modernizing Chinese

00:59:43.530 --> 00:59:45.840
to the alphabetic infrastructure,

00:59:45.840 --> 00:59:48.150
it brought us to a point where possibly,

00:59:48.150 --> 00:59:50.730
their efforts are now
completely, you know,

00:59:50.730 --> 00:59:52.983
absorbed into that progress, right?

00:59:55.230 --> 00:59:57.780
- [Attendant 5] Hi, I
had a related question

00:59:57.780 --> 00:59:59.790
about neuroscience and I wondered

00:59:59.790 --> 01:00:02.790
if there's been any
studies about brain use

01:00:02.790 --> 01:00:06.330
with a character language
versus a letter language

01:00:06.330 --> 01:00:08.640
because it makes me think, you know,

01:00:08.640 --> 01:00:12.720
people are always being told
do crossword puzzles or,

01:00:12.720 --> 01:00:16.980
you know, all kinds of
puzzles to stretch your brain.

01:00:16.980 --> 01:00:18.210
And I'm thinking,

01:00:18.210 --> 01:00:22.860
someone who learns 5,000
patterns essentially

01:00:22.860 --> 01:00:27.860
that they can recognize versus
someone who has 26 things,

01:00:29.520 --> 01:00:33.690
it just seems like their
character brain would be stretched

01:00:33.690 --> 01:00:35.280
from the outset,

01:00:35.280 --> 01:00:39.480
and I just wonder if there
was any brain science on that.

01:00:39.480 --> 01:00:41.190
- There's a couple of
people that you should read

01:00:41.190 --> 01:00:44.550
and I had the pleasure
of consulting them both.

01:00:44.550 --> 01:00:46.470
One is Mark Changizi.

01:00:46.470 --> 01:00:48.660
You can find his book, I
think, "Vision Revolution."

01:00:48.660 --> 01:00:50.409
Both came out in 2010.

01:00:50.409 --> 01:00:53.970
The other one is (indistinct)
who's based in Paris.

01:00:53.970 --> 01:00:56.160
But I just have a
personal anecdote to that.

01:00:56.160 --> 01:00:59.730
So when I read Chinese,
I can read it very,

01:00:59.730 --> 01:01:02.130
very quickly and a lot of times,

01:01:02.130 --> 01:01:04.443
I don't read character
by character at all.

01:01:05.370 --> 01:01:06.810
I can actually scan a page

01:01:06.810 --> 01:01:10.380
and kinda tell what the
content is just because

01:01:10.380 --> 01:01:15.380
that visual patterning has
become so familiar to me.

01:01:15.420 --> 01:01:18.150
When I read English, I
can't read it the same way.

01:01:18.150 --> 01:01:18.990
I can read it fast,

01:01:18.990 --> 01:01:21.150
but it's still kinda like,
you know, scanning fast.

01:01:21.150 --> 01:01:24.630
I can't just kind of as a, so
you say, just kind of like,

01:01:24.630 --> 01:01:26.250
I can't just shine.

01:01:26.250 --> 01:01:27.660
I can just shoot a glance at it

01:01:27.660 --> 01:01:30.450
as though I'm just shining
a spotlight down the page

01:01:30.450 --> 01:01:33.570
and get like a gist of
approximately what's the content.

01:01:33.570 --> 01:01:34.403
Yeah.

01:01:38.160 --> 01:01:39.767
- [Priya] So I think
there was somebody there?

01:01:39.767 --> 01:01:42.147
- [Attendant 6] Yeah. I can talk loud.

01:01:42.147 --> 01:01:45.429
- [Priya] No, no, no.
Microphone is coming.

01:01:45.429 --> 01:01:46.740
- [Attendant 6] Thank you so much.

01:01:46.740 --> 01:01:50.853
Thank you for the
wonderful, inspiring talk.

01:01:52.794 --> 01:01:56.730
I wanted to know what the
emergence of this technology did

01:01:56.730 --> 01:01:59.940
to the art of handwriting,

01:01:59.940 --> 01:02:04.620
whether as an art form or on daily basis

01:02:04.620 --> 01:02:07.620
to various generations.

01:02:07.620 --> 01:02:12.510
I assume as it was the
case in the Middle East,

01:02:12.510 --> 01:02:17.510
that was a very pressured
and beautiful art form

01:02:18.240 --> 01:02:21.390
and we can see it in our family histories,

01:02:21.390 --> 01:02:24.660
how our writing kind of degenerated

01:02:24.660 --> 01:02:27.477
from one generation to another.

01:02:27.477 --> 01:02:30.390
- I'm so glad you asked that
because it's very common,

01:02:30.390 --> 01:02:34.830
with every technological
innovation, there's a lament,

01:02:34.830 --> 01:02:37.833
a nostalgia for something
that's gonna be lost.

01:02:38.700 --> 01:02:41.070
And it's clear that certainly,

01:02:41.070 --> 01:02:43.893
I know my handwriting has
deteriorated at this age,

01:02:45.090 --> 01:02:48.130
and I think that's a very
general lament as echoed

01:02:49.620 --> 01:02:51.420
to educators around the world,

01:02:51.420 --> 01:02:52.920
but I think the digital era has, also,

01:02:52.920 --> 01:02:54.270
brought us something more, right?

01:02:54.270 --> 01:02:57.570
So I do think it is true that
some art will be lost just

01:02:57.570 --> 01:02:58.530
as when we look back,

01:02:58.530 --> 01:03:01.080
there are many customs and
practice and art forms associated

01:03:01.080 --> 01:03:02.880
with writing that we don't even use.

01:03:02.880 --> 01:03:07.170
Even like poetry, Chinese
Literati used to write verse

01:03:07.170 --> 01:03:09.960
whenever they see a friend,
when they have drinks together,

01:03:09.960 --> 01:03:12.090
they can just dash up a verse like that.

01:03:12.090 --> 01:03:14.370
For us, it'll be something
you probably have to work at

01:03:14.370 --> 01:03:16.680
for two months, if not longer, right?

01:03:16.680 --> 01:03:21.680
So I do think those habits,
technology does change,

01:03:22.380 --> 01:03:23.850
I think, what we consider to be art,

01:03:23.850 --> 01:03:27.180
but I think it's also
challenging, more importantly,

01:03:27.180 --> 01:03:31.080
what innovation means and
what creativity means, right?

01:03:31.080 --> 01:03:33.900
I'm personally very interested
in the idea of, you know,

01:03:33.900 --> 01:03:37.380
this new literacy provided by the Googles

01:03:37.380 --> 01:03:38.670
and the Wikipedias.

01:03:38.670 --> 01:03:40.380
Like, what does it come down to

01:03:40.380 --> 01:03:44.940
for knowledge is kinda gradually
replaced by information?

01:03:44.940 --> 01:03:48.300
And that there's not a place
for wisdom and experience

01:03:48.300 --> 01:03:50.790
because everything as in
wiki in the Wikipedia,

01:03:50.790 --> 01:03:53.550
it means quick in Hawaiian
which is where it's from,

01:03:53.550 --> 01:03:54.990
like as in the wiki wiki bus

01:03:54.990 --> 01:03:58.440
if you've ever been to
Hawaii and take that bus

01:03:58.440 --> 01:04:00.210
from the airport.

01:04:00.210 --> 01:04:03.420
So, you know, we have a
completely different relationship

01:04:03.420 --> 01:04:05.970
to what it means to know.

01:04:05.970 --> 01:04:08.460
It's about, what's on my fingertips,

01:04:08.460 --> 01:04:10.260
what's the first to search, you know,

01:04:10.260 --> 01:04:12.720
the first three things that
might come up when I search

01:04:12.720 --> 01:04:13.590
for something.

01:04:13.590 --> 01:04:15.930
And I think that's actually
terribly frightening

01:04:15.930 --> 01:04:18.390
'cause I certainly know that as a scholar,

01:04:18.390 --> 01:04:20.160
as a humanist scholar,

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:24.930
my greatest inspiration is to
wonder through stacks and find

01:04:24.930 --> 01:04:28.200
that accidental book that's
kind of someone misshelved

01:04:28.200 --> 01:04:30.060
and kinda takes me on a different kind

01:04:30.060 --> 01:04:34.170
of associational process thinking process.

01:04:34.170 --> 01:04:38.430
But now that associational
process has been done for you

01:04:38.430 --> 01:04:41.790
and they try to generate
randomness into it,

01:04:41.790 --> 01:04:44.310
but that randomness where it's
almost like we're not working

01:04:44.310 --> 01:04:45.143
for it.

01:04:45.143 --> 01:04:46.920
We're just like typing it in,
letting the algorithm do it.

01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:50.760
So I do think it's a kind
of strange leveling process

01:04:50.760 --> 01:04:52.170
and frankly, as a teacher, I don't know

01:04:52.170 --> 01:04:53.310
if Priya feels the same,

01:04:53.310 --> 01:04:56.085
I don't know what it means
for future generation.

01:04:56.085 --> 01:04:57.003
- [Priya] (indistinct)
For individual creativity.

01:04:57.003 --> 01:04:58.143
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:04:58.143 --> 01:05:00.269
- [Priya] It's the role
of the individual mind

01:05:00.269 --> 01:05:02.997
because you're all mining
the collective mind

01:05:02.997 --> 01:05:04.747
at this point, right?

01:05:06.027 --> 01:05:08.103
But I had a quick question, if I may.

01:05:09.030 --> 01:05:11.340
You know, so how did this impact,

01:05:11.340 --> 01:05:14.340
so the artistic tradition of calligraphy,

01:05:14.340 --> 01:05:17.370
was that really considered
as sort of high culture

01:05:17.370 --> 01:05:19.173
and sort of a separate question,

01:05:20.010 --> 01:05:24.090
sort of insulated from all
this sort of experimentation

01:05:24.090 --> 01:05:27.060
with characters?

01:05:27.060 --> 01:05:28.830
- Well, it's interesting you
say that because it also has

01:05:28.830 --> 01:05:30.750
to do with how do you
preserve calligraphy,

01:05:30.750 --> 01:05:33.720
which is so highly stylized
and individualized, right?

01:05:33.720 --> 01:05:38.160
It's almost like your signatures,
so like your fingerprint.

01:05:38.160 --> 01:05:40.620
And how that would enter
the digital age means how

01:05:40.620 --> 01:05:44.070
to represent it in font, right, in fonts.

01:05:44.070 --> 01:05:46.470
Now, when we talk about
fonts, you know, helvetica,

01:05:46.470 --> 01:05:48.690
you know, garamond,
these ones that you see.

01:05:48.690 --> 01:05:53.280
I use Calibri, I think.
But you won't believe this.

01:05:53.280 --> 01:05:55.500
I mean, you might now that
you've heard this talk,

01:05:55.500 --> 01:05:59.790
but in China, font design is huge

01:05:59.790 --> 01:06:02.400
and they have managed to design these,

01:06:02.400 --> 01:06:04.950
you know, really funky fonts all over.

01:06:04.950 --> 01:06:07.210
So much so that last year,
I believe it was last year

01:06:07.210 --> 01:06:11.700
or the year before, the
government issued a restriction,

01:06:11.700 --> 01:06:13.620
admonishing people because as, you know,

01:06:13.620 --> 01:06:16.440
these are just done by like,
you know, moving vectors,

01:06:16.440 --> 01:06:18.630
you know, dragging this
and that using software.

01:06:18.630 --> 01:06:22.170
It's not done through real
practice of calligraphy, right?

01:06:22.170 --> 01:06:24.153
People are just randomly
fattening up this character

01:06:24.153 --> 01:06:26.160
in a different like, as it was done like,

01:06:26.160 --> 01:06:27.180
a particular handwriting,

01:06:27.180 --> 01:06:29.250
but it's all just computer generated.

01:06:29.250 --> 01:06:30.630
So there's actually kind
of just as, you know,

01:06:30.630 --> 01:06:34.470
China's a hyper, I think,
technology friendly country.

01:06:34.470 --> 01:06:37.290
But at the same time,
remember, it can do both

01:06:37.290 --> 01:06:40.950
and it can understand the good

01:06:40.950 --> 01:06:45.950
of modernization,
Westernization, technology,

01:06:46.200 --> 01:06:50.430
but it also recognizes that
how it got itself this far was

01:06:50.430 --> 01:06:53.940
to preserve its cultural
identity and its cultural core.

01:06:53.940 --> 01:06:56.910
- And in many ways,
China was, one can say,

01:06:56.910 --> 01:06:59.820
much more successful
than places like India

01:06:59.820 --> 01:07:03.540
where we have a multitude
of languages but, you know,

01:07:03.540 --> 01:07:07.230
colonialism really
pushed things out a bit.

01:07:07.230 --> 01:07:10.020
It has had its advantages
in terms of, you know,

01:07:10.020 --> 01:07:11.580
Indians learning how to speak English

01:07:11.580 --> 01:07:14.550
and joining the Western
world much more seamlessly,

01:07:14.550 --> 01:07:15.690
but it's interesting

01:07:15.690 --> 01:07:20.263
that the British colonial
infrastructure did not get rid

01:07:23.910 --> 01:07:28.910
of sort of this imposition of
the alphabet and the language.

01:07:30.090 --> 01:07:32.768
Didn't have as much of a success.

01:07:32.768 --> 01:07:34.560
- You know, China watched very closely.

01:07:34.560 --> 01:07:35.393
- Yeah.

01:07:35.393 --> 01:07:39.390
- It'd always cite what was
happening in India, Persia,

01:07:39.390 --> 01:07:42.030
country civilization
that lost their language

01:07:42.030 --> 01:07:43.890
when they became colonized.

01:07:43.890 --> 01:07:46.053
So you have to understand
that this is still, you know,

01:07:46.053 --> 01:07:49.470
what you hear now in the
headlines, like when China accuses,

01:07:49.470 --> 01:07:52.170
you know, Charles Wesley
imperialism, American pluralism,

01:07:52.170 --> 01:07:55.110
it's not just rhetoric, right?

01:07:55.110 --> 01:07:57.660
It has basically ingrained
this way of thinking

01:07:57.660 --> 01:08:00.000
that this is the one thing, right,

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:02.820
that it must guard itself
against and it goes back.

01:08:02.820 --> 01:08:05.320
It goes way back. It's not
just the past 60 years.

01:08:07.650 --> 01:08:10.680
- We have time for maybe one more question

01:08:10.680 --> 01:08:12.483
if anybody wants to ask.

01:08:19.140 --> 01:08:20.250
- [Attendant 7] Hi, professor.

01:08:20.250 --> 01:08:22.600
I have a rather technical question

01:08:25.440 --> 01:08:27.660
about Lin Yutang's typewriter.

01:08:27.660 --> 01:08:32.660
So you mentioned that you have
to press two keys together

01:08:33.000 --> 01:08:35.100
for the two parts,

01:08:35.100 --> 01:08:39.750
but there's a lot of characters
that don't have two parts.

01:08:39.750 --> 01:08:41.880
They're just one piece.

01:08:41.880 --> 01:08:45.020
And so, how do you do with that?

01:08:45.020 --> 01:08:48.210
- So what he recognized, and
thank you for that question,

01:08:48.210 --> 01:08:50.790
is that it's not so much
you divide into two parts.

01:08:50.790 --> 01:08:55.740
You need to just identify the top contour

01:08:55.740 --> 01:08:57.153
and the bottom contour.

01:08:57.990 --> 01:09:01.230
So it's kinda like the four corner system

01:09:01.230 --> 01:09:02.820
where you kinda identify shape.

01:09:02.820 --> 01:09:04.380
It's shape based.

01:09:04.380 --> 01:09:05.250
So it's not necessarily true

01:09:05.250 --> 01:09:06.930
that you can only share similar parts

01:09:06.930 --> 01:09:09.960
because that will still be
a very unmanageable number.

01:09:09.960 --> 01:09:14.040
But for instance, if your
top part is a stroke across,

01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:17.490
many characters have that
as a starting stroke, right?

01:09:17.490 --> 01:09:20.130
And same with the bottom
stroke, what sort of starts it?

01:09:20.130 --> 01:09:22.920
So he actually makes it more elemental

01:09:22.920 --> 01:09:25.482
than just looking at
in terms of components.

01:09:25.482 --> 01:09:27.090
- [Attendant 7] I see. Then, okay.

01:09:27.090 --> 01:09:31.890
I guess, why use top and bottom
instead of left and right?

01:09:31.890 --> 01:09:32.723
- Sorry?

01:09:32.723 --> 01:09:33.556
- [Attendant 7] Why top and bottom

01:09:33.556 --> 01:09:35.370
instead of left and right?

01:09:35.370 --> 01:09:37.530
- Statistically, that's
how he figured it out,

01:09:37.530 --> 01:09:39.930
that it's actually not what
we think of as in, you know,

01:09:39.930 --> 01:09:41.370
like two parts side by side,

01:09:41.370 --> 01:09:43.800
which accounts for like,
top and bottom and side

01:09:43.800 --> 01:09:46.650
by side counts for the
majority shape pattern

01:09:46.650 --> 01:09:49.200
of Chinese character
writing but he figured out

01:09:49.200 --> 01:09:52.080
that the top bottom is the
easiest for his purposes.

01:09:52.080 --> 01:09:52.913
- [Attendant 7] That's interesting.

01:09:52.913 --> 01:09:54.660
- Which is all the more extraordinary,

01:09:54.660 --> 01:09:56.913
like how he arrived at this, right?

01:09:58.170 --> 01:09:59.550
- [Attendant 8] First mover.

01:09:59.550 --> 01:10:00.383
- Sorry?

01:10:00.383 --> 01:10:01.393
- [Attendant 8] He was the first mover.

01:10:01.393 --> 01:10:04.435
- He was the first mover,
but then, you know,

01:10:04.435 --> 01:10:05.430
there've been so many others.

01:10:05.430 --> 01:10:07.620
It's really a collective
effort, it feels like.

01:10:07.620 --> 01:10:08.640
People are indebted to him,

01:10:08.640 --> 01:10:10.230
but they have also gone beyond him.

01:10:10.230 --> 01:10:13.540
But nonetheless, yeah,
that was the opening salvo.

01:10:13.540 --> 01:10:15.090
- [Priya] Great.

01:10:15.090 --> 01:10:18.070
At this point, let's thank Jing
Tsu for this fantastic talk.

01:10:18.070 --> 01:10:22.710
- Thank you for having me.
(audience clapping)

01:10:25.024 --> 01:10:27.691
(gentle jingle)

